<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Carolina</h1><p class="st-meta">1663 CE – 1712 CE · capital Charles Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The proprietary colony of eight English lords, whose planned feudal constitution by John Locke gave way to a plantation society centred on Charles Town.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1663 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a> granted the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> to eight Lords Proprietors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ashley-cooper-1st-earl-of-shaftesbury">Anthony Ashley Cooper</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-hyde-1st-earl-of-clarendon">Earl of Clarendon</a>. Settlers from Virginia had already reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albemarle-sound">Albemarle Sound</a> region in the north, and in 1670 an expedition largely from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charles Town</a>, which moved to its present site in 1680.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Fundamental Constitutions of 1669, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ashley-cooper-1st-earl-of-shaftesbury">Ashley Cooper</a> and his secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, proposed a hereditary nobility but were never fully applied. Planters from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a> brought the slave plantation system, and a large trade in deerskins and in enslaved Native people grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> became the leading crop in the south from the 1690s, while the distant northern settlements had their own deputy governor and assembly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-war">Tuscarora War</a> of 1711 exposed the weakness of the divided colony, and in 1712 the proprietors gave the two parts separate governors, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-north-carolina">Province of North Carolina</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-south-carolina">Province of South Carolina</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-carolina.jpg" alt="Province of Carolina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1663 CE – 1712 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charles Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Sayle, Joseph West, Edward Hyde</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carolina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus=province-of-carolina" aria-label="Province of Carolina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2615 912 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Carolina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2935 1168L2815 1323M2935 1168L4970 663M2935 1168L4997 651M2935 1168L3048 1164M2935 1168L2936 1201"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2815 1323h0M4970 663h0M4997 651h0M3048 1164h0M2936 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2935 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2935 1168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus=province-of-carolina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

